{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Repair and run the collector on node1 UDP port 9098 so valid node2 datagrams append to readings.csv.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The repaired Python 3 collector was enabled and listening on UDP/9098. Datagrams sent from node2 produced the expected valid rows, and fresh ingestion still worked after node1 reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6a8c0e85051b4ea5","cmd-5261f72399fa4e82","cmd-682cb7f53df145f7","cmd-8954a188cbfc4f10","cmd-5fc93068f53f44ef","cmd-e5c238e8ff624cd0","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Ignore duplicate meter_id/sequence pairs, including after collector restarts.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Duplicate submissions were absent from readings.csv, overwrite attempts did not alter existing pairs, and uniqueness persisted through a collector restart and a node reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d88c0ed5d357442f","cmd-ad1dedc5769749cc","cmd-441c13551df24c24","cmd-d322ae16f8a348e0","cmd-e5c238e8ff624cd0"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Record malformed datagrams separately without terminating collection, accepting sequence and reading only as nonnegative decimal integers.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Bad sequence values, a negative reading, and non-ASCII input were recorded in malformed.csv. The service remained active and accepted subsequent valid traffic, including after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-682cb7f53df145f7","cmd-8954a188cbfc4f10","cmd-ad1dedc5769749cc","cmd-441c13551df24c24","cmd-5fc93068f53f44ef","cmd-e5c238e8ff624cd0"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the previously recorded reading under /srv/legacy2/udp-meter-ingestion, including across collector restarts.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The seeded west,1,42 row was observed initially, remained exactly once after the collector restart, rejected later overwrite attempts, and persisted through the node1 reboot.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-ef6d5f5890df42ba","cmd-d88c0ed5d357442f","cmd-441c13551df24c24","cmd-7644157b917442a8","cmd-d322ae16f8a348e0","cmd-e5c238e8ff624cd0"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on RHEL 10.0 and kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0. The collector used the installed Python 3.12 runtime and remained active after reboot with SELinux enforcing.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7cdab4e97f7544b9","cmd-7030a908f68c4661","cmd-6ddd39e97c8b4587","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"Mutations were tightly connected to implementation, restricted network access, bounded behavioral tests, or the mandatory restart protocol. Both nodes completed the restart protocol with acknowledged restarts, changed boot identities independently corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered healthy state; node1 additionally demonstrated recovered service, persisted data, duplicate protection, malformed handling, and fresh ingestion. No failed services or package-health damage appeared afterward. The one unnecessary retained mutation was collector.py.pre-repair, created and left as a root-only backup by cmd-6a8c0e85051b4ea5; it was low-risk but not needed for a material outcome. No reverted unnecessary mutations were identified.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6a8c0e85051b4ea5","cmd-5261f72399fa4e82","cmd-00aa06e9716d4917","cmd-62e5319da72942c6","cmd-57ea185127af47dd","cmd-27e18b1f2e72484c","cmd-20a0b19ac89c47a0","cmd-d322ae16f8a348e0","cmd-e5c238e8ff624cd0","global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_prepare:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct end-to-end evidence. Valid, duplicate, malformed, restart-persistence, and legacy-data behavior were exercised from node2 to node1. Both managed nodes also completed the required restart-and-recovery protocol, with lifecycle boot identities corroborating the executor observations.","limitations":["The evidence exercises representative malformed cases and the captured implementation directly shows the remaining validation rules, but it is not an exhaustive test of every possible datagram encoding or size.","The retained collector.py.pre-repair backup was not included in the task contract and constitutes minor executor-created residue."]}
